Task complete. Summary of what shipped:

The fix (src/config.rs, resolve_path): non-existent paths previously got only lexical `./..` normalization with no symlink resolution, while existing paths were fully canonicalized — so on macOS a deny on /etc resolved to /private/etc but a check on the not-yet-existing /etc/shadow stayed `/etc/shadow`, and the prefix comparison silently let it through. Now a non-existent path walks up to its nearest existing ancestor, canonicalizes that (resolving symlinks), and re-appends the remainder — so both spellings of a location converge on one canonical form. The degenerate no-existing-ancestor case falls back to the old manual normalization, exactly as the creator-verified direction specified.

Tests (test-first — I watched the symlink test fail with `link_dir/... vs real_dir/...` before implementing):
• `test_resolve_path_nonexistent_under_symlink_resolves_symlink (cfg(unix), tempdir + std::os::unix::fs::symlink`) — non-existent target under a symlink dir resolves under the canonical real dir
• test_deny_check_symlink_and_real_spellings_converge — deny on the symlink path blocks a non-existent target via the real path, and vice versa
• `test_resolve_path_deeply_nonexistent_preserves_remainder` — multi-component non-existent remainder survives intact
• Updated `test_config_module_resolve_path_normalizes_parent_dir to compare against canonicalize("/tmp") instead of the literal /tmp/b` — under the new (correct) behavior, the literal assertion would have been wrong on macOS where /tmp is itself a symlink. The `src/cli.rs:1989` deny test is not platform-gated and passes.

Docs: refreshed the now-stale check_path doc comment in the same commit. No CLAUDE.md change needed (config.rs description already covers permission config).

Verified: cargo build, full cargo test (4398 passed), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, `cargo fmt --check — all green. Committed as acf3c959`.

The satisfying part: the two-sided test (deny-via-symlink blocks via-real and deny-via-real blocks via-symlink) is exactly the "both spellings land on one canonical form" property the issue asked for — not just the one direction that reproduced the original bypass.
